## A forgotten allocator in Unix V4  

While cleaning a storage room, staff at Bell Labs uncovered a tape that held the source code for the fourth edition of Unix, dated November 1973. The find was surprising because the tape had been thought lost for decades. The recovered code shows how the early Unix team handled memory allocation in a way that feels both primitive and oddly elegant compared to today’s sprawling malloc implementations.  

The post walks through two pieces of that code: a tiny linear allocator used by the C “compiler” tools, and a region-based `malloc`/`mfree` pair that predates the modern `malloc`/`free` API. By the end, readers will see concrete examples of how the design choices reduced complexity, why the code avoided freeing memory, and what modern low-level projects can still learn from them.  

## The bump-pointer allocator in the compiler  

In the V4 source tree, the file `/usr/c/c20.c` contains a function named `alloc`. The code, reproduced below, is only a dozen lines long:

```c
alloc(an) {
    register int n;
    register char *p;
    n = an;
    n++;
    n &= ~01;
    if (lasta + n >= lastr) {
        if (sbrk(2000) == -1) {
            write(2, "Out of space\n", 14);
            exit(1);
        }
        lastr =+ 2000;
    }
    p = lasta;
    lasta =+ n;
    return(p);
}
```

The routine does three things:

1. Round up the requested size `an` to the next even boundary (`n &= ~01`).  
2. Check whether the current region (`lasta` … `lastr`) can satisfy the request.  
3. Extend the heap by 2000 bytes with `sbrk` if needed; on failure it writes an error and aborts.

Because the allocator only moves the “top” pointer forward, any pointer returned earlier remains valid after a `sbrk` call. Callers never need to test for `NULL`; the program aborts instead of returning a bad pointer. This is a classic linear (bump-pointer) allocator: allocate, never free, and rely on process termination to reclaim everything. The source does not contain a free-list implementation, keeping the code tiny and predictable.  

## Explicit memory maps in the early `malloc`  

A few directories later, the file `/usr/sys/malloc.c` defines a different interface. It introduces a `struct map` and two functions, `malloc` and `mfree`:

```c
struct map { char *m_size; char *m_addr; };
malloc(mp, size)  struct map *mp;
mfree(mp, size, aa)  struct map *mp;
```

Both functions receive a pointer to a `struct map` as their first argument. The calling code looks like this:

```c
p = malloc(coremap, sizeof foo);
...
mfree(coremap, sizeof foo, p);
q = malloc(swapmap, sizeof bar);
...
mfree(swapmap, sizeof bar, q);
```

The symbols `coremap` and `swapmap` are defined in `/usr/sys/systm.h` as simple integer arrays:

```c
int coremap[CMAPSIZ];
int swapmap[SMAPSIZ];
```

In other words, the allocator works on explicit memory regions supplied by the caller. The size argument passed to `mfree` mirrors the size given to `malloc`, making deallocation straightforward. The original author called this interface “infinitely-times better” than the modern `malloc`/`free` signature because the region can be discarded wholesale, limiting the impact of heap corruption and allowing precise control over how much memory a subsystem may consume.  

## Why freeing was left out  

Both allocators share a common philosophy: no individual frees. The bump-pointer allocator in the compiler never calls `free`; every allocation lives for the entire execution of the program. When the process exits, the operating system reclaims the whole heap automatically. This eliminates bookkeeping, reduces code size, and sidesteps fragmentation entirely.  

The region-based `malloc`/`mfree` pair also avoids per-object frees. Instead of returning a single block, a subsystem can release an entire map in one step, effectively freeing all its objects at once. The source does not contain any free-list logic, suggesting that the designers prioritized simplicity and predictability over the flexibility of later general-purpose allocators.  

## Trade-offs and legacy impact  

The early design makes several trade-offs clear:

* Predictable performance, Allocation is a few arithmetic operations and, occasionally, a `sbrk`. No search through free lists means constant-time behavior.  
* Memory waste, Because memory is never reclaimed until the process ends or a whole region is discarded, long-running programs could hold onto unused blocks.  
* Safety, Bulk region release limits the blast radius of a stray write; corrupting one region does not affect another.  
* Limited lifetimes, The model works best when objects share the same lifetime as the program or as a well-defined subsystem.  

Modern systems still use variants of these ideas. Bump allocators appear in game engines and embedded firmware where allocation patterns are short-lived. Region-based allocators (sometimes called arena allocators) are common in compilers and parsers that need to allocate many small objects and then free them all at once.  

## The takeaway  

Early Unix memory management favored tiny, predictable allocators that never needed to free individual blocks. The linear bump-pointer allocator in the compiler and the explicit region maps in `malloc.c` illustrate a design where the lifetime of memory is tied to either the whole process or a well-defined region. When building low-level tools, consider whether a simple bump allocator or a region-based scheme can replace a full-featured heap, especially when the allocation pattern is short-lived or easily partitioned.  

*Source: [The lost malloc()](https://napcakes.nekoweb.org/posts/malloc/)*
